# Solution

## Setup

Let $V = \prod_{k=1}^{2021} k^k$. Each day we divide by $2021!$. After $d$ days, the value is $V/(2021!)^d$, which is an integer iff $v_p(V) \geq d \cdot v_p(2021!)$ for every prime $p \leq 2021$.

Define the ratio $R_p = v_p(V)/v_p(2021!)$. The maximum number of complete integer divisions is:
$$d_{\max} = \min_p \lfloor R_p \rfloor$$

On day $d_{\max}+1$, the result becomes a non-integer fraction $a/b$. The prime factors of $b$ are exactly those primes with $R_p < d_{\max}+1$, i.e., $\lfloor R_p \rfloor = d_{\max}$.

## Computing the ratio

Using the identity $v_p(V) = \sum_{j\geq 1} p^j \cdot T(n_j)$ where $T(n)=n(n+1)/2$ and $n_j = \lfloor 2021/p^j \rfloor$, and $v_p(2021!) = \sum_{j\geq 1} n_j$:

**Key observation:** The $j$-th term contributes average $A_j = p^j(n_j+1)/2$ to the weighted mean $R_p$. Since $p^j n_j \leq 2021 < p^j(n_j+1)$, we get $p^j(n_j+1) \geq 2022$, so $A_j \geq 1011$ for every $j$. Therefore **$R_p \geq 1011$ for all primes $p$**.

Equality $R_p = 1011$ holds iff every active term satisfies $p^j(n_j+1) = 2022$, which requires $p \mid 2022 = 2 \times 3 \times 337$.

## Checking candidate primes

**Primes dividing 2022** ($p \in \{2, 3, 337\}$, giving $j=1$ average $= 1011$):

- **$p=2$:** $n_1=1010, n_2=505, \ldots, n_{10}=1$. The $j=2$ term has $4 \times 506 = 2024$, so $A_2 = 1012 \neq 1011$. Computing: $v_2(V) = 2038090$, $v_2(2021!) = 2013$, $R_2 = 2038090/2013 \approx 1012.46$. So $\lfloor R_2 \rfloor = 1012$.

- **$p=3$:** $n_1=673, n_2=224, \ldots$. The $j=2$ term has $9 \times 225 = 2025$, so $A_2 = 1012.5 \neq 1011$. Computing: $v_3(V) = 1017463$, $v_3(2021!) = 1005$, $R_3 = 1017463/1005 \approx 1012.40$. So $\lfloor R_3 \rfloor = 1012$.

- **$p=337$:** $n_1 = 5$ (since $337 \times 6 = 2022 > 2021$), $n_2 = 0$ (since $337^2 > 2021$). Only $j=1$ active: $v_{337}(V) = 337 \cdot 15 = 5055$, $v_{337}(2021!) = 5$, $R_{337} = 5055/5 = \mathbf{1011}$.

**Primes dividing 2023** ($2023 = 7 \times 17^2$, giving $j=1$ average $= 1011.5$):

- **$p=7$:** $n_1=288, n_2=41, n_3=5$. Higher terms push average up: $R_7 = 338646/334 \approx 1013.91$. $\lfloor R_7 \rfloor = 1013$.

- **$p=17$:** $n_1=118, n_2=6, n_3=0$. The $j=2$ term: $289 \times 7 = 2023$, so $A_2 = 1011.5 = A_1$. Both terms have the same average! $v_{17}(V) = 17 \cdot 7021 + 289 \cdot 21 = 119357 + 6069 = 125426$, $v_{17}(2021!) = 124$, $R_{17} = 125426/124 = \mathbf{1011.5}$.

**All other primes** have $p(n_1+1) \geq 2024$, so $j=1$ average $\geq 1012$. I verified that no prime other than $p=17$ has a higher-order term ($j \geq 2$) with average $< 1012$ (this would require $p^j \mid 2022$ or $p^j \mid 2023$ for $j \geq 2$, and the only solution is $p=17, j=2$). So all higher-order terms also have average $\geq 1012$, giving $R_p \geq 1012$.

## Determining $d_{\max}$ and the denominator

$$d_{\max} = \min_p \lfloor R_p \rfloor = 1011 \quad \text{(achieved by } p=17 \text{ and } p=337\text{)}$$

After $1011$ divisions, the result is still an integer (verified: $v_{337}(V) = 5055 = 1011 \times 5$, $v_{17}(V) = 125426 \geq 1011 \times 124 = 125364$).

On day $1012$, we divide once more. The negative valuations are:
- $p = 337$: $5055 - 1012 \times 5 = -5$
- $p = 17$: $125426 - 1012 \times 124 = 125426 - 125488 = -62$

All other primes have $R_p \geq 1012$, so their valuations remain non-negative.

The denominator $b = 17^{62} \times 337^5$, whose distinct prime factors are **17** and **337**.

## Answer

$$17 + 337 = \boxed{354}$$

